WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:11.268
One fall afternoon Mr. Harrison and Anne were having afternoon tea when Mr. Harrison inquired, "Been writing any more stories lately, Anne?"

2
00:00:11.269 --> 00:00:14.697
"No," answered Anne rather curtly.

3
00:00:14.698 --> 00:00:23.177
"I heard that someone recently sent a big envelope to the Rollings Reliable Baking Company in Montreal," remarked Mr. Harrison.

4
00:00:23.178 --> 00:00:28.762
"I thought maybe it was you, sending in your story to the Rollings Reliable Story Competition."

5
00:00:28.763 --> 00:00:33.007
"I would never do that!" replied Anne indignantly.

6
00:00:33.008 --> 00:00:40.260
"I read about the competition, but I think it's disgraceful to write a story to advertise a baking powder.

7
00:00:40.261 --> 00:00:46.210
It's almost as bad as Mr. Parker's fence with advertising all over it!"

8
00:00:46.211 --> 00:00:50.672
When Anne said that, she had no idea of how the day would unfold.

9
00:00:50.673 --> 00:00:56.803
That very evening, Diana popped into Green Gables. She was carrying a letter.

10
00:00:56.804 --> 00:01:00.880
"Anne, open this letter right away!" she said excitedly.

11
00:01:00.881 --> 00:01:03.918
"I was at the post office, so I picked it up for you.

12
00:01:03.919 --> 00:01:07.176
I have a feeling it's good news."

13
00:01:07.177 --> 00:01:12.143
Puzzled, Anne opened the letter. "Dear Madam," she read.

14
00:01:12.144 --> 00:01:23.314
"We take much pleasure in informing you that your charming story 'Averil's Atonement' has won the first prize of $25 in our recent story competition.

15
00:01:23.315 --> 00:01:27.711
Regards, Rollings Reliable Baking Company.

16
00:01:27.712 --> 00:01:31.312
"I don't understand," said Anne blankly.

17
00:01:31.313 --> 00:01:36.887
Diana clapped her hands. "Oh, I knew it would win the prize! I was sure of it.

18
00:01:36.888 --> 00:01:40.165
I sent your story into the competition, Anne."

19
00:01:40.166 --> 00:01:43.640
"Diana Barry!" Anne exclaimed.

20
00:01:43.641 --> 00:01:49.767
Diana was not always the most observant person, but she realized that Anne was not overjoyed.

21
00:01:49.768 --> 00:01:53.246
"Why, Anne, you don't seem a bit pleased."

22
00:01:53.247 --> 00:02:02.280
Anne instantly made herself smile. "Diana," Anne began, "of course I'm touched that you helped me get my story published.

23
00:02:02.281 --> 00:02:13.766
That was very unselfish of you, but . . . there wasn't a word in my story about . . . about . . ." Anne choked a little over the word, "baking powder!"

24
00:02:13.767 --> 00:02:21.978
"Oh, I put that in," said Diana. "It was easy! You know the scene where Averil makes the cake?

25
00:02:21.979 --> 00:02:28.167
Well, I just added in that she used Rollings Reliable Baking Powder, and that's why the cake was so delicious.

26
00:02:28.168 --> 00:02:31.834
And in the final scene, after Averil and Harold kiss . . ."

27
00:02:31.835 --> 00:02:35.575
"Yes?" said Anne quietly.

28
00:02:35.576 --> 00:02:44.164
"I wrote, 'and they always used Rollings Reliable Baking Powder in every cake, and lived happily ever after,'" Diana quoted proudly.

29
00:02:44.165 --> 00:02:49.152
"Oh," gasped Anne, as if someone had thrown cold water on her.

30
00:02:49.153 --> 00:02:54.299
"And you've won twenty-five dollars," continued Diana jubilantly.

31
00:02:54.300 --> 00:03:00.905
"Why, I heard that magazines only pay five dollars a story! I'm so happy for you, Anne."

32
00:03:00.906 --> 00:03:04.811
Anne held the hateful check that had been attached to the letter.

33
00:03:04.812 --> 00:03:15.463
"I can't take it, Diana. You deserve it, since you sent the story in and made the changes. I . . . I wouldn't have sent it."

34
00:03:15.464 --> 00:03:20.463
"Absolutely not! I would never take that money; it's yours, Anne!

35
00:03:20.464 --> 00:03:25.797
The honor of being a friend of the prizewinner is enough for me," Diana said.

36
00:03:25.798 --> 00:03:31.192
Anne suddenly bent forward, put her arms around Diana, and kissed her cheek. "I

37
00:03:31.193 --> 00:03:38.064
think you are the sweetest and truest friend in the world, Diana. Thank you." Anne

38
00:03:38.065 --> 00:03:40.828
left with tears in her eyes. She

39
00:03:40.829 --> 00:03:45.596
felt deeply embarrassed over what had happened to her literary endeavor. "I

40
00:03:45.597 --> 00:03:51.984
will never live this down," she thought. "Never!" A

41
00:03:51.985 --> 00:03:55.304
week later Anne left for Halifax. For

42
00:03:55.305 --> 00:04:01.680
the first time, she was not sorry to leave Avonlea because her last week there had not been pleasant. Her

43
00:04:01.681 --> 00:04:07.913
story was published in all the newspapers on Prince Edward Island, and everyone had an opinion. Josie

44
00:04:07.914 --> 00:04:19.172
Pye was sure that Anne had copied the story while Mrs. Lynde said, unimpressed, "It's perfectly amazing, the price they pay for such lies!" Back

45
00:04:19.173 --> 00:04:26.157
in Halifax Anne, Priscilla, Stella, and Phil settled quickly into their new home at Patty's Place. Mrs.

46
00:04:26.158 --> 00:04:36.076
Lynde and Marilla had given Anne jams and cakes and other foods, so the girls had a full pantry. And soon they were welcoming visitors. They

47
00:04:36.077 --> 00:04:42.918
passed many enjoyable Friday evenings entertaining guests around the fireplace in their cozy living room. Gilbert

48
00:04:42.919 --> 00:04:48.485
was a frequent visitor, but Anne still felt uncomfortable around him at times. It

49
00:04:48.486 --> 00:04:52.487
seemed as if their relationship had gotten very complicated. One

50
00:04:52.488 --> 00:05:01.397
afternoon Anne was sitting under the apple tree behind Patty's Place when she saw Gilbert coming toward her with a bunch of wildflowers. "Don't

51
00:05:01.398 --> 00:05:06.549
these remind you of home and our old school picnics, Anne?" he asked. Anne

52
00:05:06.550 --> 00:05:09.958
smiled and buried her face in the flowers. "I

53
00:05:09.959 --> 00:05:13.934
feel like I'm back in Avonlea right now!" "Will

54
00:05:13.935 --> 00:05:17.797
you go back to Avonlea for winter vacation?" Gilbert asked. "Yes,

55
00:05:17.798 --> 00:05:22.712
I will," Anne answered. "And I guess you will too?" "No,

56
00:05:22.713 --> 00:05:26.680
I'll be working in Halifax," said Gilbert. "Oh,"

57
00:05:26.681 --> 00:05:34.942
said Anne, surprised. She couldn't picture what a whole Avonlea winter would be like without Gilbert. "Well,

58
00:05:34.943 --> 00:05:42.444
a new job . . . that's exciting," said Anne, without any very clear idea of what she was saying. Gilbert

59
00:05:42.445 --> 00:05:46.098
was now staring at Anne with a strange expression. Anne

60
00:05:46.099 --> 00:05:50.107
wished desperately that Phil would come out and interrupt them. "Well

61
00:05:50.108 --> 00:05:57.267
. . . uh . . . I found a cluster of white violets under that old twisted tree today," Anne mumbled. "They

62
00:05:57.268 --> 00:06:02.191
were so pretty, I felt as if I'd discovered a gold mine!" "You

63
00:06:02.192 --> 00:06:06.409
are always discovering gold mines, Anne," said Gilbert. "Let's

64
00:06:06.410 --> 00:06:14.950
find some more violets. I think I saw some more over there," suggested Anne quickly. "I'll call Phil and—" "Never

65
00:06:14.951 --> 00:06:20.566
mind Phil and the violets just now, Anne," said Gilbert, quietly taking her hand. He

66
00:06:20.567 --> 00:06:25.992
looked deep into Anne's eyes. "There is something I want to say to you." "Oh,

67
00:06:25.993 --> 00:06:32.429
don't say it," cried Anne. "Don't . . . please , Gilbert," she begged him.

68
00:06:32.430 --> 00:06:38.454
"I must," Gilbert said firmly. "Things can't go on like this any longer.

69
00:06:38.455 --> 00:06:44.319
Anne, I love you. You know I do. I can't tell you how much.

70
00:06:44.320 --> 00:06:54.387
Will you promise me that one day you'll be my wife?"

